[FINAL-TEXT] The evidence is unambiguous and complete. Every single data point contradicts the ticket's claim.

**Summary of findings:**
- Router4 is fully up; FRR/RIP running with a complete routing table
- eth0 (192.168.200.1/24) — LAN gateway — responds from **all four PCs** with 0% loss
- Both WAN uplinks from router4 are live: eth5 → router3 (172.25.1.2, 0% loss), eth4 → router5 (192.168.15.1, 0% loss)
- PC11 and PC14 reach remote sites (192.168.100.1, 10.50.1.1) with 0% loss
- switch4 bridge has all ports UP

---

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket describes a catastrophic router4 failure — gateway unreachable and no WAN connectivity for PC11–PC14 — but live measurements refute every element of this claim. Router4's container is running normally, its FRR daemon is up, and the RIP routing process has converged with a full table that includes routes to all remote sites via both WAN uplinks (eth4 toward router5 at 192.168.15.1 and eth5 toward router3 at 172.25.1.2). The LAN-facing interface eth0 (192.168.200.1/24) is UP with link and responds to ICMP from all four clients (PC11 through PC14) with 0% packet loss. PC11 and PC14 both reach cross-site prefixes (192.168.100.1 and 10.50.1.1) with 0% loss, and a traceroute from PC11 confirms the expected two-hop path through router4 and router3. Switch4 (the L2 device bridging PC11–PC14 to router4) has all ports in the UP/forwarding state. There is no fault in this topology. The ticket is a false-premise report: the users appear to have panicked and submitted an "emergency" based on a transient perceived failure or a misread of their workstation state. No corrective action on network equipment is required; the on-call engineer should verify the end-user devices directly and close the ticket as a false alarm.
=== END ===